' The body of operator-api-events.puml / operator-api-events-dark.puml. Not a
' diagram on its own: it carries no start marker, so PlantUML will not render
' this file directly.
'
' Careful: the start and end markers must not appear in this file *at all*, not
' even inside a comment like this one. PlantUML scans for them lexically, so a
' comment that merely names one opens a second diagram here and the include
' fails with a line number pointing at neither.
'
' Split out because OPERATOR-API.md embeds a light and a dark render and they
' must not drift. Everything the same in both lives here; each wrapper adds only
' the colours that differ. See README.md for why a global flag cannot do this.

' A post-render step widens this single font name into a fallback stack, so a
' viewer without Inter gets a sans-serif rather than the monospace a missing
' font can otherwise fall back to.
skinparam defaultFontName Inter
skinparam componentStyle rectangle
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam nodesep 16
skinparam ranksep 44
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam packageStyle rectangle

title The live channel: four bridges, one hub, two transports

header
<b>An upsert is a create and an update collapsed into one.</b>  Right for a subscriber rebuilding a view,
wrong for one reacting to arrivals, so the bridges track seen ids and split it back apart here,
rather than reshaping a wire format several services depend on around one consumer.
Event names and payloads: <b>OPERATOR-API.md</b> S6.   Request path: <b>operator-api-request.puml</b>.
endheader

package "**upstream services**, gRPC streams" as UP $c_up {
  component "**session-view**\nSubscribe: snapshot · upsert · gone" as sview $c_seed
  component "**metadata**\nGetTree to seed, then Watch\n**decode limit raised**: an imported\ntree outgrows gRPC's 4 MiB default" as meta $c_seed
  component "**text**\nWatch: delivered messages" as text $c_card
  component "**context-actions**\nWatch: entries owner=operator-api" as ctx $c_card
}

package "**starling-operator-api**" as API $c_api {
  component "**bridge_sessions**\nseen ids -> userConnected\nlast state -> userStateChanged\n«suppressed when the projection is equal»\nremoved -> userDisconnected, last state" as bsess $c_card
  component "**bridge_channels**\nseeded from the tree first, so an existing\nchannel's first edit is not a creation\n-> channelCreated · channelStateChanged\n· channelRemoved" as bchan $c_card
  component "**bridge_text**\n-> userTextMessage" as btext $c_card
  component "**bridge_context**\n-> contextAction" as bctx $c_card

  component "**EventHub**\ntokio broadcast, **1024 deep**\nattached counter, 4 of 4 = live\n--\n**not a replay.** Read /v1/sessions and\n/v1/channels first, then follow this" as hub $c_hub

  component "**WebSocket**  GET /v1/events\nauthorised **before** the upgrade" as ws $c_wire
  component "**WebTransport**  HTTP/3, same path\nnewline-delimited, off unless configured\nevents out · commands in, two uni streams" as wt $c_wire
}

actor "subscriber\nviewer · user manager · bot" as sub $c_sub

sview --> bsess : upsert · gone
meta  --> bchan : upsert · removed
text  --> btext : message
ctx   --> bctx  : trigger

bsess --> hub
bchan --> hub
btext --> hub
bctx  --> hub

hub --> ws : Event
hub --> wt : Event
ws -[$c_out]-> sub : JSON per message
wt -[$c_out]-> sub : JSON per line
sub -[$c_in]-> ws : addContext · removeContext · ping
sub -[$c_in]-> wt : same commands

note right of hub $c_hub
  <b>started fires at 4 of 4, not at the first.</b>  It was set by the
  session bridge alone, so it went out while the channel bridge
  was still attaching: a channel created in that window produced
  no event, and a subscriber that had done what started invites
  waited for something nobody was listening for.
  --
  <b>stopped fires when any one drops.</b>  A subscriber cannot tell
  which half of the server it has stopped hearing about.
  --
  A subscriber arriving while the channel is already live is sent
  its own <b>started</b>, since this is published once and never replayed.
  --
  Falling behind 1024 sends <b>lagged</b> with the count, rather than
  hiding it: a consumer that knows can re-read the state routes,
  one that does not acts on a picture it believes is current.
end note

note right of wt $c_wire
  <b>The channel is bidirectional for this one reason.</b>  A context-menu
  entry has to live exactly as long as the connection servicing it,
  so a disconnect withdraws what it registered. Entries are owned by
  <b>operator-api</b>, not by the operator: operators come and go and the
  menu entry outlives the request.
end note

legend bottom
  <b>Reconnect is silent.</b>  A bridge re-seeds from the snapshot, so everyone already
  connected is not reported as an arrival and a restart is not a join flood. Retry is
  a flat 2 s, and an attach that hangs is abandoned at 5 s, without which a dial that
  never refuses means the retry is never reached and the channel silently never starts.
  --
  <b>WebSocket is the one that works everywhere.</b>  WebTransport is Extended CONNECT over
  HTTP/3, which reverse proxies do not generally forward, so a proxied deployment uses
  the WebSocket and binds no UDP port. A QUIC port that will not bind is logged, never
  fatal: it must not take down the surface carrying the same channel.
endlegend
